course: biology
lesson: 400
title: The big biology quiz, part 4: everything from lessons 301 to 399
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: This is the big quiz for the last hundred lessons, so it gathers the main ideas once more: counting legs, toes, teeth and blowholes, the animals of Ukraine and China, sleep, poisonous plants and cartoons put right.
explain: Counting is a superpower in biology. Six legs means an insect, eight means a spider or a tick. Eighteen toes means a real cat, not a cartoon one. Two blowholes means a baleen whale, one means a toothed whale.
explain: Bodies are built for their lives: an owl swings one toe back to grip struggling prey, a dolphin sleeps with half its brain so it can keep coming up to breathe, and your strong thigh bone carries all your weight.
explain: And some rules keep you safe for life: never eat a wild berry, leaf or mushroom unless a grown-up who really knows says it is safe, and always tell a grown-up if a friend has.
fact: An owl has four toes on each foot, and one outer toe can swing forward or back, so it can grip with two in front and two behind; owls cannot move their eyes in their sockets, so they turn their heads instead.
fact: All insects have six legs and three body parts, so a beetle, an ant and a bee have eighteen legs together; a spider has eight legs, so it is not an insect.
fact: The sperm whale and the orca have teeth, while the blue whale and the humpback have baleen plates for straining small food from the water; baleen whales have two blowholes and toothed whales have one.
fact: A full set of grown-up teeth is 32 and a full set of baby teeth is 20; the last four grown-up teeth, the wisdom teeth, come in late, and some people never get them.
fact: The longest bone in the body is the thigh bone, or femur, about a quarter of your height, and the smallest bone is in your ear and is smaller than a grain of rice.
fact: The safe rule for the wild is never to eat any berry, leaf or mushroom unless a grown-up who really knows says it is safe; many poisonous plants look tasty, many poisonous mushrooms look plain, and silver spoon or colour tests are myths.
fact: The white stork nests on poles and chimneys in Ukrainian villages, builds huge stick nests it uses year after year, flies to Africa for the winter and returns in spring; many Ukrainians see it as a sign of good luck and home.
fact: The silkworm, a caterpillar that eats mulberry leaves, spins silk for its cocoon; people in China have kept silkworms for thousands of years, and the domestic silk moth can no longer live in the wild.
fact: A silkworm has six true, pointed legs at the front, like every insect, and ten soft stumpy legs further back.
fact: Gigantopithecus was a giant ape from southern China known mostly from teeth, which are very hard and last longer than other bones, first found sold as dragon teeth in medicine shops; caves later gave many more, but no full skeleton has been found, so nobody knows its exact height.
fact: A dolphin sleeps with half its brain at a time and keeps coming up to breathe, with one eye open controlled by the awake half; ducks at the edge of a sleeping row can do the same.
fact: A real cat has five toes on each front paw and four on each back paw, eighteen in all, while cartoon cats like Tom are often drawn with four fingers; real cats cannot talk, wear clothes or bounce back from being flattened.
exercise: How many toes does an owl have on each foot? | Four | Three | Five | Two
exercise: Why can an owl grip with two toes in front and two behind? | One outer toe can swing forward or back | Its toes are glued together in pairs by its feathers | It has two feet on each leg | Its toes grow backwards in winter
exercise: Why does an owl turn its head so much? | It cannot move its eyes in their sockets | It has no neck bones at all, so its head can spin round | It is always dizzy | Its ears are on its back
exercise: How many legs do a beetle, an ant and a bee have together? | Eighteen | Twelve | Twenty-four | Nine
exercise: Why is a spider not an insect? | It has eight legs, not six | It has no legs at all, like a worm | It can spin silk from its body | It lives only inside people's houses
exercise: Which whale has teeth? | The sperm whale | The giant blue whale | The humpback whale | All whales have teeth
exercise: How many blowholes does a blue whale have? | Two | One | Three | None
exercise: How many teeth are in a full set of grown-up teeth? | 32 | 20 | 28 | 40
exercise: How many teeth are in a full set of baby teeth? | 20 | 32 | 10 | 24
exercise: What is the longest bone in your body? | The thigh bone, or femur | The long backbone | The upper arm bone | The round skull
exercise: Where is the smallest bone in your body? | In your ear | In your little toe | In your nose | In your finger tip
exercise: What is the safe rule for wild berries and mushrooms? | Never eat any unless a grown-up who really knows says it is safe | Eat only red berries, and test every mushroom first with a silver spoon | Test mushrooms with a silver spoon | Eat what birds and slugs eat
exercise: Which big bird nests on poles and chimneys in Ukrainian villages? | The white stork | The golden eagle | The big white swan | The tall grey heron
exercise: Where do white storks from Ukraine spend the winter? | In Africa | In the Arctic | On the same chimney | In northern China
exercise: What does a silkworm eat? | Mulberry leaves | Oak leaves | Grass | Small flies
exercise: Which giant ape is known mostly from teeth sold as dragon teeth? | Gigantopithecus | The gorilla | The orangutan | Bigfoot
exercise: How does a dolphin sleep without drowning? | With half its brain at a time, coming up to breathe | It swims up onto the beach and sleeps on the sand | It holds its breath for a whole night | It sleeps floating on its back like an otter, holding hands
exercise: How many toes does a real cat have? | Eighteen | Sixteen | Twenty | Twelve
test: «my brother says spiders are insects cos they're creepy crawlies» What is the best correction? | Spiders have eight legs, insects have six, so spiders are not insects | Right, all crawling things are insects, whatever number of legs they have | Spiders are insects without wings | Spiders are tiny crabs
test: Count them: a beetle and a spider sit on a leaf. How many legs together? | 14 | 12 | 16 | 18
test: «baby teeth, grown-up teeth, whatever, how many more do you get» How many more grown-up teeth than baby teeth in full sets? | 12 | 10 | 20 | 8
test: Count them: 3 owls perch on a branch. How many toes in all? | 24 | 12 | 18 | 30
test: Why do you think the longest and strongest bone is in your leg? | It carries your whole weight when you stand, walk and jump | Legs need to be long so that people look tall | Arms are too short to hold long bones | Bones always grow longest in the part of the body where it is warmest
test: «it's October, where'd the stork go, did it die» What is the best answer? | It flew to Africa for the winter and should return in spring | Storks sleep inside the warm chimneys all winter long | Storks turn into geese in autumn | Storks hide under the ice of the ponds
test: Count them: a silkworm has 6 pointed true legs and 10 soft stumpy ones. How many legs on 2 silkworms? | 32 | 16 | 12 | 20
test: Why are teeth found as fossils more often than other bones? | Teeth are very hard and last longer than other bones | Animals used to have far more teeth than they had bones | Teeth were buried on purpose | Teeth float to the surface
test: What would happen if a dolphin fell fully asleep like a person, with its whole brain at once? | It could forget to come up for air and drown | It would sleep better than ever and wake up full of energy | It would sink and breathe water | Nothing, dolphins can breathe under water
test: In Tom and Jerry, Tom is flattened by a piano and pops back to shape. What is true of a real cat? | A real cat would be badly hurt and could not bounce back | Real cats bounce back like rubber | Real cats are never hurt by falls | Real cats can talk to the piano player
test: «a friend ate some shiny black berries from the park, should we just not tell anyone?» What is the right thing to do? | Tell a grown-up straight away | Keep it secret so nobody is cross | Give the friend more berries to compare | Wait until tomorrow to see what happens
test: Which animal sleeps standing up much of the time? | A horse | A dolphin | A fruit bat | A pet cat
exam: It is given that each insect possesses six legs. Ascertain the aggregate number of legs belonging to one beetle, one ant and one bee. | Eighteen | Twelve | Twenty-four | Nine
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the distinction between baleen whales and toothed whales. | Baleen whales strain small food from the water and possess two blowholes, whereas toothed whales possess one | Toothed whales possess two blowholes, whereas baleen whales possess one, and both groups consume large prey | Both groups possess teeth and a single blowhole | Baleen whales consume large prey with their teeth
exam: Identify the bone of the human body which is of the greatest length. | The femur, or thigh bone | The bone of the upper arm | The spinal column | The smallest bone of the ear
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which a dolphin sleeps? | It sleeps with one half of its brain at a time, rising periodically to breathe | It sleeps continuously upon the floor of the sea | It sleeps upon the shore during the hours of darkness | It does not sleep at any time
